) let  \n\n\\[\nd_{1}<d_{2}<d_{3}\n\\]\n\nbe the three smallest proper divisors larger than \\(1\\).  \nThen the three largest proper divisors are \\(\\frac{n}{d_{1}},\\frac{n}{d_{2}},\\frac{n}{d_{3}}\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)=\\frac{n}{d_{1}}+\\frac{n}{d_{2}}+\\frac{n}{d_{3}}\n      =n\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (odd numbers decrease).  \n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd and \\(\\tau(n)\\ge4\\) then \\(f(n)<n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(n\\) is odd, its smallest proper divisor \\(>1\\) is at least \\(3\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\n   \\le\\frac13+\\frac15+\\frac17\n   =\\frac{71}{105}<1 .\n\\]\n\nBy (1) we obtain \\(f(n)=n\\bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\bigr)<n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (absence of the factor\u202f3 forces decrease).  \n\nLet \\(n\\) be even, \\(3\\nmid n\\), and \\(\\tau(n)\\ge4\\). Then \\(f(n)<n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(4\\mid n\\) the three smallest divisors are \\(2,3,4\\); otherwise they are\n\\(2,p,q\\) with \\(p\\ge5\\). In both cases  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\n   \\le\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15\n   =\\frac{19}{20}<1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(f(n)<n\\) by (1). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (any factor\u202f5 leads to an odd term).  \n\nAssume \\(5\\mid n\\) and \\(\\tau(n)\\ge4\\). Then after finitely many applications of \\(f\\) an odd integer appears.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(4\\mid n\\) the three smallest divisors are \\(2,3,4\\) and\n\\(f(n)=\\frac{13}{12}n\\). This operation reduces the exponent of \\(2\\) by \\(2\\) while preserving the factor \\(5\\). Repeating it \\(\\lfloor\\alpha/2\\rfloor\\) times (where \\(\\alpha\\) is the exponent of \\(2\\) in \\(n\\)) we obtain a number \\(n'\\) with exponent of \\(2\\) equal to \\(1\\) and still divisible by \\(5\\).  \n\nNow the three smallest divisors are \\(2,3,5\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(n')=\\frac{31}{30}n' .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(30\\mid n'\\), the quotient \\(\\frac{n'}{30}\\) is an integer and the factor \\(31\\) is odd; thus \\(f(n')\\) is odd. By Lemma\u202f1 the subsequent terms cannot stay in the required set. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Consequences of the lemmas  \n\n* From Lemma\u202f1, any admissible \\(a_{1}\\) must be even.  \n* Lemma\u202f2 forces the presence of the factor \\(3\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f3 forbids the factor \\(5\\).  \n\nHence any admissible integer can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=2^{\\alpha}3^{\\beta}m,\n\\qquad\\alpha\\ge1,\\;\\beta\\ge1,\\;\nm\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Parity of the exponent of\u202f2  \n\nIf \\(\\alpha\\) is even, write \\(\\alpha=2t\\).  \nApplying the rule for numbers divisible by \\(4\\) (see below) \\(t\\) times removes the factor \\(2\\) completely, producing an odd integer \u2013 impossible by Lemma\u202f1.  \nTherefore \\(\\alpha\\) must be odd; set  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha=2k+1\\qquad(k\\ge0). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Behaviour when \\(4\\mid n\\)  \n\nFor a number \\(n\\) with \\(4\\mid n\\) and \\(3\\mid n\\) (the situation forced by (2) and (3)),\nthe three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,4\\).  \nFrom (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)=\\frac{13}{12}n}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4) reduces the exponent of \\(2\\) by \\(2\\) and multiplies the number by \\(13\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Reaching a fixed point  \n\nStarting from (2) with \\(\\alpha=2k+1\\) we apply (4) exactly \\(k\\) times.  \nAfter the \\(i\\)-th application (\\(0\\le i\\le k\\)) we have  \n\n\\[\na_{i+1}=2^{2k+1-2i}\\,3^{k+1}\\,13^{\\,i}\\,m\n      =6\\cdot12^{\\,k-i}\\cdot13^{\\,i}m . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(i=k\\) the exponent of \\(2\\) becomes \\(1\\); (5) yields  \n\n\\[\na_{k+1}=6\\cdot13^{\\,k}m .\n\\]\n\nNow the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,6\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(a_{k+1})=a_{k+1},\n\\]\n\nso the sequence is constant from this point onward.  \nThus every integer of the form  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=6\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m},\n\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nproduces an infinite sequence all of whose terms have at least three proper divisors.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Exhaustiveness  \n\nConversely, let \\(a_{1}\\) be any integer with \\(\\tau(a_{1})\\ge4\\) that does **not** satisfy (6).  \nBy Lemmas\u202f1\u20133 one of the following occurs:\n\n* \\(a_{1}\\) is odd \u2013 Lemma\u202f1 forces a decrease to a non\u2011admissible term.\n* \\(a_{1}\\) lacks the factor \\(3\\) \u2013 Lemma\u202f2 forces a decrease.\n* \\(a_{1}\\) contains the factor \\(5\\) \u2013 Lemma\u202f3 eventually yields an odd term.\n* The exponent of \\(2\\) in \\(a_{1}\\) is even \u2013 repeated use of (4) eliminates the factor \\(2\\) and produces an odd term.\n\nIn each case the sequence leaves the set \\(\\{n:\\tau(n)\\ge4\\}\\) after finitely many steps, contradicting the requirement that the sequence be infinite. Hence no integer outside the family (6) can serve as \\(a_{1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nAll and only the numbers described in (6) can be chosen as \\(a_{1}\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=6\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m\\quad(k\\ge0,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m)} .\n\\]"
