eaching a fixed point.**  \n   Starting from (2) with \\(\\alpha=2k+1\\) and applying (3) exactly \\(k\\) times we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a_{k+1}=6\\cdot13^{\\,k}\\,m .\n   \\]\n\n   For this number the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,6\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   f(a_{k+1})=a_{k+1},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the sequence becomes constant.  \n   Every intermediate term still possesses at least three proper divisors (they contain \\(2,3\\) and either \\(4\\) or \\(6\\)).\n\n6. **Construction.**  \n   For any integers \\(k\\ge0\\) and odd \\(m\\) with \\(5\\nmid m\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   a_{1}=6\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m=2^{2k+1}3^{k+1}m .\n   \\]\n\n   Induction using (3) shows that the sequence generated from this \\(a_{1}\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   a_{i+1}=6\\cdot12^{\\,k-i}\\cdot13^{\\,i}m\\qquad(0\\le i\\le k),\n   \\]\n\n   and thereafter remains constant at \\(6\\cdot13^{k}m\\). Hence every such \\(a_{1}\\) indeed yields an infinite admissible sequence.\n\n7. **Exhaustiveness.**  \n   Lemmas\u202f1\u20133 together with the parity argument on the exponent of \\(2\\) force any admissible \\(a_{1}\\) to have the form (2) with \\(\\alpha\\) odd, i.e. exactly the numbers described in step\u202f6. No other integer can start a valid sequence.\n\nThus the boxed expression lists **all** possible values of \\(a_{1}\\).\n\n---  \n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\text{sum of the three largest proper divisors of }n .\n\\]\n\nFor a composite integer \\(n\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\nd_{1}<d_{2}<d_{3}\n\\]\n\nits three smallest proper divisors larger than \\(1\\).  \nThe three largest proper divisors are \\(\\frac{n}{d_{1}},\\frac{n}{d_{2}},\\frac{n}{d_{3}}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)=\\frac{n}{d_{1}}+\\frac{n}{d_{2}}+\\frac{n}{d_{3}}\n      =n\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (odd numbers decrease)\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd and \\(\\tau(n)\\ge4\\) then \\(f(n)<n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(n\\) is odd, its smallest proper divisor exceeding \\(1\\) is at least \\(3\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\n   \\le\\frac13+\\frac15+\\frac17=\\frac{71}{105}<1 .\n\\]\n\nBy (1) we obtain \\(f(n)=n\\bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\bigr)<n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (absence of the factor\u202f3 forces decrease)\n\nLet \\(n\\) be even, \\(3\\nmid n\\), and \\(\\tau(n)\\ge4\\). Then \\(f(n)<n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(4\\mid n\\) the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,4\\) and the smallest odd divisor \\(p\\ge5\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\n   \\le\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac{19}{20}<1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(4\\nmid n\\) the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,p,q\\) with \\(p\\ge5\\) and \\(q\\ge p\\); then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\n   \\le\\frac12+\\frac15+\\frac15=\\frac{9}{10}<1 .\n\\]\n\nIn either case (1) gives \\(f(n)<n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (a factor\u202f5 inevitably produces an odd term)\n\nAssume \\(5\\mid n\\) and \\(\\tau(n)\\ge4\\). Then after finitely many applications of \\(f\\) an odd integer appears.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 we must have \\(3\\mid n\\). Write \\(n=2^{\\alpha}3^{\\beta}5^{\\gamma}m\\) with \\(\\alpha\\ge1,\\;\\beta,\\gamma\\ge1,\\;m\\) odd and \\(\\gcd(m,30)=1\\).\n\nWhile \\(\\alpha\\ge2\\) the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,4\\); by (1)  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\frac{13}{12}n .\n\\]\n\nEach application reduces \\(\\alpha\\) by \\(2\\) and leaves the factor \\(5\\) unchanged. After \\(\\lfloor\\alpha/2\\rfloor\\) steps we obtain a number \\(n'\\) with exponent of \\(2\\) equal to \\(1\\); its three smallest proper divisors are now \\(2,3,5\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(n')=\\frac{31}{30}n' .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(30\\mid n'\\), the quotient \\(n'/30\\) is odd; multiplying by \\(31\\) yields an odd integer. By Lemma\u202f1 this odd term forces the sequence to leave the set \\(\\{n:\\tau(n)\\ge4\\}\\). Hence a starting integer containing the factor \\(5\\) cannot generate an infinite admissible sequence. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequences of the lemmas  \n\n* From Lemma\u202f1 any admissible \\(a_{1}\\) must be even.  \n* Lemma\u202f2 forces the presence of the factor \\(3\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f3 forbids the factor \\(5\\).\n\nHence every admissible integer can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=2^{\\alpha}3^{\\beta}m,\\qquad\\alpha\\ge1,\\;\\beta\\ge1,\\;\nm\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Parity of the exponent of\u202f2  \n\nIf \\(\\alpha\\) were even, applying (3) (see below) repeatedly would eventually eliminate the factor \\(2\\) and produce an odd term, contradicting Lemma\u202f1. Therefore \\(\\alpha\\) must be odd; write  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha=2k+1\\qquad(k\\ge0). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Behaviour when \\(4\\mid n\\)\n\nUnder the conditions (2) and (3) the three smallest proper divisors of \\(n\\) are \\(2,3,4\\). Substituting \\(d_{1}=2,\\;d_{2}=3,\\;d_{3}=4\\) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)=\\frac{13}{12}n}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4) reduces the exponent of \\(2\\) by \\(2\\) and multiplies the number by \\(13\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reaching a fixed point  \n\nStarting from (2) with \\(\\alpha=2k+1\\) and applying (4) exactly \\(k\\) times we obtain  \n\n\\[\na_{k+1}=2^{1}3^{1}13^{\\,k}m=6\\cdot13^{\\,k}m .\n\\]\n\nFor this number the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,6\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(a_{k+1})=a_{k+1},\n\\]\n\nso the sequence becomes constant from this point onward.  \nEvery intermediate term still possesses at least three proper divisors (they contain \\(2,3\\) and either \\(4\\) or \\(6\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction  \n\nFor any integers \\(k\\ge0\\) and odd \\(m\\) with \\(5\\nmid m\\) define  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=6\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m=2^{2k+1}3^{k+1}m .\n\\]\n\nInduction using (4) yields  \n\n\\[\na_{i+1}=6\\cdot12^{\\,k-i}\\cdot13^{\\,i}m\\qquad(0\\le i\\le k),\n\\]\n\nand \\(a_{k+1}=6\\cdot13^{k}m\\) is a fixed point. Hence every such \\(a_{1}\\) indeed generates an infinite sequence all of whose terms have at least three proper divisors.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness  \n\nLemmas\u202f1\u20133 together with the parity argument on the exponent of \\(2\\) force any admissible \\(a_{1}\\) to have the form (2) with \\(\\alpha\\) odd, i.e. precisely the numbers  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=6\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m,\\qquad k\\ge0,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m .\n\\]\n\nNo integer outside this family can start a valid sequence, completing the classification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=6\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m\\qquad(k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m)} .\n\\]"
