s? For odd N, the triple (p,q,r) cannot include 2, so sum of reciprocals is less than 1, so f(N) < N, so no fixed points. So odd numbers cannot be fixed points. So indeed odd numbers are excluded.\n\nThus the solution's elimination of odd numbers is valid.\n\nNow they eliminate numbers divisible by 30 but not by 4 because they map to odd numbers (via (2,3,5) case), which are impossible. So those are excluded.\n\nNow they eliminate numbers divisible by 12 but whose quotient N/12 is not a fixed point. They argue that if N=12k, then f(N)=13k. If k odd, 13k odd, impossible. If k even, 13k even but not divisible by 3 (since k not multiple of 3 unless k is fixed point). They claim an even number not divisible by 3 can never acquire factor 3 later, so cannot reach fixed point. Let's examine that claim.\n\nIf we have an even number not divisible by 3, can it ever become divisible by 3 in subsequent steps? The recurrence is a_{n+1} = sum of three largest proper divisors of a_n. If a_n is even but not divisible by 3, then its divisors may include 2, maybe other divisors. The three largest proper divisors are N/p, N/q, N/r where p,q,r are three smallest divisors >1. Since N is even, p=2. Since N is not divisible by 3, q is the smallest divisor >1 after 2, which could be something else (maybe 4 if N divisible by 4, else some odd divisor). The sum of reciprocals 1/2+1/q+1/r may be less than 1? Possibly. But can the resulting a_{n+1} be divisible by 3? Let's examine: a_{n+1} = N*(1/2+1/q+1/r). Since N is not divisible by 3, for a_{n+1} to be divisible by 3, the factor (1/2+1/q+1/r) must introduce a factor 3 in denominator that cancels something? Actually a_{n+1} is integer, so N*(1/2+1/q+1/r) = N*(something rational). The denominator of that rational is lcm(p,q,r). Since p=2, q,r are divisors of N. So denominator divides N. So a_{n+1} = N * (some integer)/lcm(p,q,r). Since lcm(p,q,r) divides N (since p,q,r divide N), the result is integer. But the factor may have a factor 3 in nu