llest divisors >1 equal to those triples can increase or stay same. Others will decrease.\n\nThus for any number N that does not have three smallest divisors >1 equal to one of those triples, we have a_{n+1} <= N - 1. So the sequence will strictly decrease until it possibly hits a number with one of those triples. So eventually the sequence will either reach a fixed point (2,3,6) or a number that yields (2,3,4) or (2,3,5) and then increase. But if it increases, we need to see if it can lead to infinite sequence.\n\nNow we need to classify numbers that have three smallest divisors >1 equal to (2,3,6). That's the fixed point case. Those numbers are N = 6*m where m odd, 5 ∤ m, exponent of 2 exactly 1. That's the set F.\n\nNow numbers that have three smallest divisors >1 equal to (2,3,4) are numbers divisible by 12 but not by 5? Actually (2,3,4) means N divisible by 2,3,4. Since 4 = 2^2, N must be divisible by 2^2 and 3, i.e., divisible by 12. Also N must not be divisible by any integer between 4 and something else that would be smaller than 4? Actually the three smallest divisors >1 are 2,3,4. That means N is divisible by 2,3,4, but not by any integer d such that 2 < d < 3 (none), or 3 < d < 4 (none), or 4 < d < something else? Actually the next divisor after 4 could be 5, but if N is divisible by 5, then the three smallest divisors >1 would be 2,3,5 (since 5 < 4? No, 5 > 4, so the three smallest >1 would be 2,3,4 still, because 4 is smaller than 5. So having extra divisors >4 does not affect the three smallest. So the condition for (2,3,4) is that N is divisible by 2,3,4, i.e., divisible by 12, and also that N is not divisible by any divisor between 2 and 3 (none) or between 3 and 4 (none). So any N divisible by 12 qualifies for (2,3,4) as the three smallest >1? Wait, we need to check if N divisible by 2^2 = 4, but also divisible by 3. So N divisible by 12. However, what about N divisible by 5? That would not affect the three smallest >1 because 5 > 4. So the three sm