ors: 1,2,3,4,6,12. Proper: 1,2,3,4,6. Three largest proper: 6,4,3 = 13. So a_2 = 13, prime. So fails. So 12 not admissible.\n\n- N=18? 18 = 6*3, m=3 odd, 5 ∤ 3, t=0 => 18 is in answer set? Wait 6*m*12^t = 6*3*1 = 18, yes. So 18 should be admissible. Let's test: N=18, divisors: 1,2,3,6,9,18. Proper: 1,2,3,6,9. Three largest proper: 9,6,3 = 18. So a_2 = 18, fixed point? Actually three smallest divisors >1: 2,3,6 => (2,3,6) case, fixed point. So works.\n\n- N=30? 30 = 6*m*12^t? 30 = 6*5, m=5 odd, but 5 ∤ m? Actually condition says 5 ∤ m, but m=5 is divisible by 5, so 30 is not allowed. Let's test N=30: Divisors: 1,2,3,5,6,10,15,30. Proper: 1,2,3,5,6,10,15. Three largest proper: 15,10,6 = 31. So a_2 = 31, prime. So fails.\n\n- N=24? Already tested fails.\n\n- N=36? 36 = 6*6, m=6 even, not allowed. 36 = 6*6 = 6*2*3? Actually m must be odd, so 36 not allowed. Let's test N=36: Divisors: 1,2,3,4,6,9,12,18,36. Proper: 1,2,3,4,6,9,12,18. Three largest proper: 18,12,9 = 39. a_2 = 39. 39 = 3*13. Divisors: 1,3,13,39. Proper: 1,3,13. Three largest proper: 13,3,1 = 17. a_3 = 17, prime. So fails.\n\n- N=48? 48 = 6*8, m=8 even, not allowed. 48 = 12*4, t=1? Actually 48 = 6*8 = 6*8*12^0, not allowed. Let's test N=48: Divisors: 1,2,3,4,6,8,12,16,24,48. Proper: 1,2,3,4,6,8,12,16,24. Three largest proper: 24,16,12 = 52. a_2 = 52 = 2^2 * 13. Divisors: 1,2,4,13,26,52. Proper: 1,2,4,13,26. Three largest proper: 26,13,4 = 43. a_3 = 43 prime. So fails.\n\n- N=54? 54 = 6*9, m=9 odd, 5 ∤ 9, t=0 => allowed. Let's test N=54: Divisors: 1,2,3,6,9,18,27,54. Proper: 1,2,3,6,9,18,27. Three largest proper: 27,18,9 = 54. So fixed point. So works.\n\n- N=72 (allowed) works.\n\n- N=90? 90 = 6*15, m=15 odd, but 5 ∤ 15? Actually 5 divides 15, so not allowed. So 90 not allowed. Let's test N=90: Divisors: 1,2,3,5,6,9,10,15,18,30,45,90. Proper: 1,2,3,5,6,9,10,15,18,30,45. Three largest proper: 45,30,18 = 93. a_2 = 93 = 3*31. Divisors: 1,3,31,93. Proper: 1,3,31. Three largest proper: 31,3,1 = 35. a_3 = 35 = 5*7