rs: 1,2,3,4,6,12. Proper: 1,2,3,4,6. Three largest proper: 6,4,3 = 13. So a_2 = 13, prime. So fails. So 12 not admissible.\n\n- N=18? 18 = 6*3, m=3 odd, 5 ∤ 3, t=0 => 18 is in answer set? Wait 6*m*12^t = 6*3*1 = 18, yes. So 18 should be admissible. Let's test: N=18, divisors: 1,2,3,6,9,18. Proper: 1,2,3,6,9. Three largest proper: 9,6,3 = 18. So a_2 = 18, fixed point? Actually three smallest divisors >1: 2,3,6 => (2,3,6) case, fixed point. So works.\n\n- N=30? 30 = 6*m*12^t? 30 = 6*5, m=5 odd, but 5 ∤ m? Actually condition says 5 ∤ m, but m=5 is divisible by 5, so 30 is not allowed. Let's test N=30: Divisors: 1,2,3,5,6,10,15,30. Proper: 1,2,3,5,6,10,15. Three largest proper: 15,10,6 = 31. So a_2 = 31, prime. So fails.\n\n- N=24? Already tested fails.\n\n- N=36? 36 = 6*6, m=6 even, not allowed. 36 = 6*6 = 6*2*3? Actually m must be odd, so 36 not allowed. Let's test N=36: Divisors: 1,2,3,4,6,9,12,18,36. Proper: 1,2,3,4,6,9,12,18. Three largest proper: 18,12,9 = 39. a_2 = 39. 39 = 3*13. Divisors: 1,3,13,39. Proper: 1,3,13. Three largest proper: 13,3,1 = 17. a_3 = 17, prime. So fails.\n\n- N=48? 48 = 6*8, m=8 even, not allowed. 48 = 12*4, t=1? Actually 48 = 6*8 = 6*8*12^0, not allowed. Let's test N=48: Divisors: 1,2,3,4,6,8,12,16,24,48. Proper: 1,2,3,4,6,8,12,16,24. Three largest proper: 24,16,12 = 52. a_2 = 52 = 2^2 * 13. Divisors: 1,2,4,13,26,52. Proper: 1,2,4,13,26. Three largest proper: 26,13,4 = 43. a_3 = 43 prime. So fails.\n\n- N=54? 54 = 6*9, m=9 odd, 5 ∤ 9, t=0 => allowed. Let's test N=54: Divisors: 1,2,3,6,9,18,27,54. Proper: 1,2,3,6,9,18,27. Three largest proper: 27,18,9 = 54. So fixed point. So works.\n\n- N=72 (allowed) works.\n\n- N=90? 90 = 6*15, m=15 odd, but 5 ∤ 15? Actually 5 divides 15, so not allowed. So 90 not allowed. Let's test N=90: Divisors: 1,2,3,5,6,9,10,15,18,30,45,90. Proper: 1,2,3,5,6,9,10,15,18,30,45. Three largest proper: 45,30,18 = 93. a_2 = 93 = 3*31. Divisors: 1,3,31,93. Proper: 1,3,31. Three largest proper: 31,3,1 = 35. a_3 = 35 = 5*7. Divisors: 1,5,7,35. Proper: 1,5,7. Three largest proper: 7,5,1 = 13. a_4 = 13 prime. So fails.\n\n- N=108? 108 = 6*18, m=18 even, not allowed. But 108 = 6*3*12? Actually 108 = 6*3*12^1? Let's see: 12^1 = 12, 6*12 = 72, times 3 = 216? No. So 108 not of the form 6*m*12^t with m odd. Let's test N=108: Divisors: 1,2,3,4,6,9,12,18,27,36,54,108. Proper: 1,2,3,4,6,9,12,18,27,36,54. Three largest proper: 54,36,27 = 117. a_2 = 117 = 3*3*13? Actually 117 = 9*13 = 3^2 * 13. Divisors: 1,3,9,13,39,117. Proper: 1,3,9,13,39. Three largest proper: 39,13,9 = 61. a_3 = 61 prime. So fails.\n\nThus far, all numbers not of the form 6*m*12^t (with m odd, 5 ∤ m) fail. So the answer set seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to check if any numbers of the form 6*m*12^t with m odd, 5 ∤ m, t≥0 could fail. Let's test some random ones:\n\n- t=0, m odd, 5 ∤ m: m=1 => 6 works. m=3 => 18 works. m=7 => 42? Wait 6*7 = 42. 42 = 2*3*7. Divisors: 1,2,3,6,7,14,21,42. Proper: 1,2,3,6,7,14,21. Three largest proper: 21,14,7 = 42. So fixed point. So 42 works. Indeed 42 = 6*m with m odd, 5 ∤ m. So works.\n\n- m=9 => 54 works. m=11 => 66 works? 66 = 2*3*11. Divisors: 1,2,3,6,11,22,33,66. Proper: 1,2,3,6,11,22,33. Three largest proper: 33,22,11 = 66. So fixed point. So works.\n\n- m=13 => 78 works (we saw). m=15 (divisible by 5) not allowed. m=17 => 102 works? 102 = 2*3*17. Divisors: 1,2,3,6,17,34,51,102. Proper: 1,2,3,6,17,34,51. Largest three: 51,34,17 = 102. So fixed point. So all numbers of the form 6*m with m odd, 5 ∤ m are fixed points.\n\nNow t≥1: N = 6*m*12^t. For t=1, N = 72*m. For m odd, 5 ∤ m. For m=1, N=72 works. For m=3, N=216? Actually 6*3*12 = 216. Let's test N=216: Divisible by 12, a_2 = (13/12)*216 = 13*18 = 234. 234 = 2*3*3*13? Actually 234 = 2*3*3*13 = 2*3^2*13. Divisors: smallest >1: 2,3,4? Actually 4 does not divide 234 (since 2^2=4, 234/4 = 58.5). So the three smallest >1 are 2,3,6? Actually 6 divides 234 (2*3). So the three smallest >1 are 2,3,6. So (2,3,6) case => fixed point. So after a_2, 