{\\alpha}3^{\\beta}r,\\qquad \n   \\alpha\\ge1\\text{ odd},\\ \\beta\\ge\\frac{\\alpha+1}{2},\\ \\gcd(r,30)=1,\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   then the iteration described in steps\u202f5\u20137 produces a finite number of strictly increasing terms (each of which is a multiple of \\(6\\), thus has at least three proper divisors) and then a fixed point. Hence the infinite sequence exists.\n\n9. **Necessity.**  \n   Conversely, any \\(a_1\\) not of the form (6) falls into one of the excluded cases handled in steps\u202f2\u20134 or violates condition (5). In each such case the factor in (1) is \\(<1\\) at some stage, forcing a strict decrease that eventually yields a term with fewer than three proper divisors. Hence no infinite sequence can arise.\n\n**Conclusion:** The set of all possible initial values \\(a_1\\) is exactly the set described in (6), i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_1 = 2^{\\alpha}3^{\\beta}r\\ \\text{with}\\ \\alpha\\ge1\\ \\text{odd},\\ \\beta\\ge\\frac{\\alpha+1}{2},\\ \\gcd(r,30)=1 } .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_1 = 6\\cdot2^{\\,2k}\\cdot3^{\\,\\ell}\\cdot r,\\qquad k,\\ell\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\ \\ell\\ge k,\\ \\gcd(r,30)=1 } .\n\\]  \n\nAll such numbers generate an infinite sequence, and no other starting value does.  \n\n---  \n\n**Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\text{the sum of the three largest proper divisors of }N .\n\\]\n\nThe sequence is defined by \\(a_{n+1}=f(a_n)\\) and each term must have at least three proper divisors, i.e. \\(\\tau(N)\\ge4\\) where \\(\\tau(N)\\) denotes the number of (positive) divisors of \\(N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Expression of \\(f(N)\\)).  \nLet \\(N\\) be a positive integer with \\(\\tau(N)\\ge4\\).  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\np_1<p_2<p_3\n\\]\n\nthe three smallest proper divisors of \\(N\\) that are larger than \\(1\\).  \nThen the three largest proper divisors of \\(N\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\frac N{p_1},\\qquad \\frac N{p_2},\\qquad \\frac N{p_3},\n\\]\n\nand consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,f(N)=N\\Bigl(\\frac1{p_1}+\\frac1{p_2}+\\frac1{p_3}\\Bigr)\\,}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(d\\) is a divisor of \\(N\\) with \\(d>1\\), then \\(N/d\\) is also a divisor and \\(N/d<N\\).  \nThe map \\(d\\mapsto N/d\\) reverses the order of the proper divisors larger than \\(1\\); therefore the three largest proper divisors correspond exactly to the three smallest proper divisors \\(>1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Immediate decrease when the smallest prime divisor is \\(\\ge5\\)).  \nIf the smallest prime divisor of \\(N\\) satisfies \\(p_1\\ge5\\), then \\(f(N)<N\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(p_1\\ge5\\) we have \\(p_2\\ge p_1\\) and \\(p_3\\ge p_2\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{p_1}+\\frac1{p_2}+\\frac1{p_3}\\le\\frac1{5}+\\frac1{5}+\\frac1{5}=\\frac35<1,\n\\]\n\nand (1) gives \\(f(N)<N\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Decrease when the smallest prime divisor is \\(3\\)).  \nIf \\(p_1=3\\) then \\(f(N)<N\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(3^2\\mid N\\) then \\(p_2=9\\) and \\(p_3\\ge9\\); otherwise \\(p_2\\ge5\\) and \\(p_3\\ge p_2\\). In both sub\u2011cases  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{3}+\\frac1{p_2}+\\frac1{p_3}\\le\\frac13+\\frac15+\\frac15=\\frac{11}{15}<1\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n\\frac13+\\frac19+\\frac19=\\frac59<1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(f(N)<N\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (If \\(2\\mid N\\) but \\(3\\nmid N\\) then \\(f(N)<N\\)).  \nAssume \\(2\\mid N\\) and \\(3\\nmid N\\). Then \\(f(N)<N\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(3\\nmid N\\), the second smallest proper divisor is at least \\(4\\); the third is at least \\(5\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac{19}{20}<1,\n\\]\n\nso (1) yields \\(f(N)<N\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemmas\u202f2\u20134 we deduce that **any infinite sequence must satisfy**  \n\n\\[\np_1=2\\quad\\text{and}\\quad3\\mid N .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus every term is a multiple of \\(6\\). Write  \n\n\\[\nN=2^{\\alpha}3^{\\beta}r,\\qquad \\alpha\\ge1,\\ \\beta\\ge1,\\ r\\text{ odd}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (Classification of the third smallest divisor).  \nFor a number \\(N\\) satisfying (2) the triple \\((p_1,p_2,p_3)\\) is one of the following:\n\n1. \\((2,3,6)\\)  iff \\(4\\nmid N\\) and \\(5\\nmid N\\);\n2. \\((2,3,5)\\)  iff \\(5\\mid N\\) and \\(4\\nmid N\\);\n3. \\((2,3,4)\\)  iff \\(4\\mid N\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(2\\mid N\\) and \\(3\\mid N\\), the divisors \\(2,3,6\\) are always present.  \nIf \\(4\\mid N\\) then \\(4<5<6\\) and the third smallest divisor is \\(4\\).  \nIf \\(4\\nmid N\\) but \\(5\\mid N\\), then \\(5<6\\) and the third smallest divisor is \\(5\\).  \nIf neither \\(4\\) nor \\(5\\) divides \\(N\\), the third smallest divisor is \\(6\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nUsing Lemma\u202f5 and (1) we obtain the three possible values of the factor  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda(N):=\\frac{f(N)}{N}=\n\\begin{cases}\n1, & (p_1,p_2,p_3)=(2,3,6),\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{31}{30}, & (2,3,5),\\\\[3mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{13}{12}, & (2,3,4).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 6 (Numbers divisible by \\(5\\) cannot start an infinite sequence).  \nIf \\(5\\mid N\\) and \\(4\\nmid N\\) then the sequence terminates after at most two steps.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn this situation \\(\\lambda(N)=31/30>1\\). Because \\(30\\mid N\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{31}{30}N=31\\cdot\\frac N{30},\n\\]\n\nwhich is odd. For an odd integer the smallest prime divisor is at least \\(3\\); by Lemma\u202f3 the next factor is \\(<1\\). Hence after the second step the sequence strictly decreases and eventually reaches a number with fewer than three proper divisors. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nHence **any admissible starting value must be not divisible by \\(5\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 7 (Behaviour in the \\((2,3,4)\\) case).  \nAssume \\(4\\mid N\\) (so \\((p_1,p_2,p_3)=(2,3,4)\\)).  \nWrite \\(N\\) as in (3) with \\(\\alpha\\ge2\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nf(N)=\\frac{13}{12}N=13\\cdot2^{\\alpha-2}3^{\\beta-1}r .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSubstituting \\((2,3,4)\\) into (1) gives \\(\\lambda(N)=13/12\\). Since \\(12\\mid N\\) (because \\(\\alpha\\ge2,\\beta\\ge1\\)), the right\u2011hand side of (5) is an integer. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nIterating (5) yields the following.\n\n#### Lemma 8 (Explicit formula under repeated \\((2,3,4)\\) steps).  \nLet \\(N\\) satisfy the hypotheses of Lemma\u202f7. For any integer \\(k\\ge0\\) with \\(\\alpha-2k\\ge2\\),\n\n\\[\nf^{\\,k}(N)=2^{\\alpha-2k}3^{\\beta-k}13^{k}r .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nInduction on \\(k\\). The case \\(k=0\\) is trivial. Assuming (6) holds for \\(k\\), apply Lemma\u202f7 to \\(f^{\\,k}(N)\\); the exponent of \\(2\\) drops by \\(2\\), that of \\(3\\) by \\(1\\), and a factor \\(13\\) is introduced, giving the formula for \\(k+1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe exponent of \\(2\\) in (6) decreases by two at each step. Two possibilities arise.\n\n*If \\(\\alpha\\) is even*, after \\(\\alpha/2\\) steps the exponent of \\(2\\) becomes \\(0\\); the resulting term is odd, and by Lemma\u202f3 the next factor is \\(<1\\). Hence the sequence cannot be infinite.\n\n*If \\(\\alpha\\) is odd*, write \\(\\alpha=2k+1\\) with \\(k\\ge0\\). After exactly \\(k\\) steps the exponent of \\(2\\) equals \\(1\\); (6) gives\n\n\\[\nN_k = f^{\\,k}(N)=2\\cdot3^{\\beta-k}13^{k}r .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 9 (Reaching a fixed point).  \nAssume \\(\\alpha\\) is odd and  \n\n\\[\n\\beta\\ge k+1=\\frac{\\alpha+1}{2}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThen the number \\(N_k\\) in (7) satisfies \\((p_1,p_2,p_3)=(2,3,6)\\); consequently \\(f(N_k)=N_k\\) and the sequence becomes constant.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause the exponent of \\(2\\) in (7) is \\(1\\), the number is not divisible by \\(4\\). Condition (8) guarantees that the exponent of \\(3\\) in (7) is at least \\(1\\), so \\(3\\mid N_k\\). By construction \\(5\\nmid r\\) and \\(13\\) is coprime to \\(5\\); thus \\(5\\nmid N_k\\). Hence the three smallest proper divisors larger than \\(1\\) are \\(2,3,6\\). Lemma\u202f5 then yields \\(\\lambda(N_k)=1\\), i.e. \\(f(N_k)=N_k\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 10 (Necessity of the inequality (8)).  \nIf \\(\\alpha\\) is odd but \\(\\beta<(\\alpha+1)/2\\), then after the \\(\\lfloor\\alpha/2\\rfloor\\) steps described in Lemma\u202f8 the exponent of \\(3\\) becomes \\(0\\). At that moment the third smallest divisor is either \\(5\\) (if \\(5\\mid\\) the current term) or at least \\(7\\); in both cases the factor \\(\\lambda\\) is \\(<1\\). Hence the sequence cannot be infinite.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWith \\(\\alpha=2k+1\\) the exponent of \\(3\\) after \\(k\\) steps equals \\(\\beta-k\\). If \\(\\beta<k+1\\) then \\(\\beta-k\\le0\\); thus the term after \\(k\\) steps is not divisible by \\(3\\). Its smallest prime divisor is at least \\(5\\) (or \\(7\\) if \\(5\\) does not divide it). In either case Lemma\u202f1 gives a factor \\(\\le\\frac12+\\frac15+\\frac15<1\\) or \\(\\le\\frac12+\\frac15+\\frac17<1\\). Consequently the sequence strictly decreases and eventually terminates. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 11 (Complete description of admissible \\(a_1\\)).  \nA positive integer \\(a_1\\) admits an infinite sequence \\((a_n)\\) defined by the problem statement **iff** it can be written as  \n\n\\[\na_1=2^{\\alpha}3^{\\beta}r,\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n* \\(\\alpha\\ge1\\) is **odd**,  \n* \\(\\beta\\ge\\displaystyle\\frac{\\alpha+1}{2}\\),  \n* \\(r\\) is odd and \\(\\gcd(r,30)=1\\) (equivalently \\(5\\nmid r\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \n*If* the three conditions hold, set \\(\\alpha=2k+1\\). By Lemma\u202f8 the first \\(k\\) iterates are given by (6); each of them is a multiple of \\(6\\) and therefore possesses at least three proper divisors. Lemma\u202f9 shows that after the \\(k\\)-th step we reach a fixed point, so the sequence is infinite.\n\n*Conversely*, suppose an infinite sequence exists. Lemmas\u202f2\u20134 force \\(2\\mid a_n\\) and \\(3\\mid a_n\\) for every \\(n\\); thus each term has the shape (3) with \\(\\alpha\\ge1,\\beta\\ge1\\). Lemma\u202f5 eliminates the possibilities \\((2,3,5)\\) and \\((2,3,6)\\) for the initial term unless it already is a fixed point. Hence the initial term must satisfy \\(4\\mid a_1\\) (otherwise we would be in the \\((2,3,5)\\) case, which Lemma\u202f6 forbids). Repeated application of Lemma\u202f7 shows that the exponent of \\(2\\) decreases by \\(2\\) each step; to avoid reaching an odd term we need \\(\\alpha\\) odd. Finally Lemma\u202f10 shows that we must have \\(\\beta\\ge(\\alpha+1)/2\\); otherwise the exponent of \\(3\\) would vanish before the exponent of \\(2\\) reaches \\(1\\), and the factor would become \\(<1\\). The condition \\(5\\nmid a_1\\) follows from Lemma\u202f6. Thus the three stated conditions are necessary. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final answer  \n\nAll admissible initial values are precisely those described in Lemma\u202f11. In compact form:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_1 = 2^{\\alpha}3^{\\beta}r\\ \\text{with}\\ \\alpha\\ge1\\ \\text{odd},\\ \n\\beta\\ge\\frac{\\alpha+1}{2},\\ \\gcd(r,30)=1 } .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_1 = 6\\cdot2^{\\,2k}\\cdot3^{\\,\\ell}\\cdot r,\\qquad \nk,\\ell\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\ \\ell\\ge k,\\ \\gcd(r,30)=1 } .\n\\]\n\nEvery number of this form generates an infinite sequence (after a finite number of steps the sequence becomes constant), and no other starting value can do so."
