r of }m\\ge7 .\n\\]\n\nConversely, any integer of this shape satisfies the required conditions:\nthe first \\(k\\) terms of the sequence are obtained by repeatedly applying\n(13), after which the term becomes the fixed point \\(13^{k}\\,2\\cdot3^{\\alpha}m\\)\nand the sequence remains constant.  Hence the infinite sequence exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nAll possible initial values are exactly the integers described above.  In\ncompact form:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=2^{2k+1}\\,3^{\\,k+\\alpha}\\,m\\quad\n(k\\ge0,\\;\\alpha\\ge1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m,\\;\n\\text{all prime factors of }m\\ge7)} .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=2\\cdot3^{\\alpha}\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m\n\\quad(k\\ge0,\\;\\alpha\\ge1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m,\\;\n\\text{all prime factors of }m\\ge7)} .\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(a_{1}\\) the sequence becomes constant after at most \\(k\\)\nsteps; for any other \\(a_{1}\\) the sequence cannot remain infinite while\nevery term possesses at least three proper divisors."
