ion. So those are allowed.\n\nNow test a1 = 2 * 3^α * m where m includes a factor 7, e.g., a1 = 2 * 3^1 * 7 = 42. Divisors: 1,2,3,6,7,14,21,42. Proper divisors: 1,2,3,6,7,14,21. The three largest proper divisors are 21,14,7 sum = 42. So indeed 42 is a fixed point. So 42 is allowed. That matches the description: α=1, m=7.\n\nNow test a1 = 2 * 3^2 * 7 = 126. Divisors: 1,2,3,6,7,9,14,18,21,42,63,126. Proper divisors: all except 126. The three largest proper divisors: 63,42,21 sum = 126. Indeed, because the three smallest >1 are 2,3,6, so sum of three largest = N. So 126 is a fixed point. So all numbers of that form are fixed points.\n\nNow test a1 = 2 * 3^α * m where m has a factor 5. For example, a1 = 2 * 3 * 5 = 30. Divisors: 1,2,3,5,6,10,15,30. Proper divisors: 1,2,3,5,6,10,15. The three largest proper divisors: 15,10,6 sum = 31. So a2 = 31 (prime). So 30 is not allowed. Indeed, the solution excludes factor 5.\n\nNow test a1 = 2 * 3^α * m where m includes a prime 11. For example, a1 = 2 * 3 * 11 = 66. Divisors: 1,2,3,6,11,22,33,66. Proper divisors: 1,2,3,6,11,22,33. The three largest proper divisors: 33,22,11 sum = 66. So 66 is a fixed point. So allowed.\n\nNow test a1 = 2 * 3^α * m where m includes a prime 13. For example, a1 = 2 * 3 * 13 = 78 (we already saw). So allowed.\n\nNow test a1 = 2 * 3^α * m where m includes a prime 7 and also a factor 5? Not allowed.\n\nNow test a1 = 2 * 3^α * m where m includes a factor 9? Actually m is odd and can include powers of primes >=7. So m can be 7^2 = 49. Then a1 = 2 * 3 * 49 = 294. Divisors: 1,2,3,6,7,14,21,42,49,98,147,294. The three largest proper divisors: 147,98,49 sum = 294. So fixed point. Good.\n\nNow test a1 = 2 * 3^α * m where m includes a factor 7^2 * 11^3 etc. All fine.\n\nNow test a1 = 2^{2k+1} * 3^{k+α} * m for k>0. For example, k=2, α=1, m=1: a1 = 2^{5} * 3^{3} = 32 * 27 = 864. Divisors: many. Let's check if it's of the form 12^k * u where u is fixed point. 12^2 = 144, u = 864/144 = 6. So a1 = 12^2 * 6. So afte