            "content": "**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;a_{1}=2\\cdot 3^{k}\\;(k\\ge 1)\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a_{1}=6p\\;(p\\text{ prime },p\\ge 7)\\;}\n\\]\n\n(The number \\(6\\) belongs to the first family with \\(k=1\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 1.  Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nAll admissible initial values are exactly the numbers  \n\n* \\(2\\cdot 3^{k}\\;(k\\ge 1)\\)   (e.g. \\(6,18,54,162,\\dots\\)), and  \n* \\(6p\\) where \\(p\\) is a prime \\(\\ge 7\\)   (e.g. \\(42,66,78,102,\\dots\\)).\n\n**b. Method sketch**  \n\n1. **Minimal‑value argument.**  \n   In any infinite sequence \\((a_n)\\) with the required property the set\n   \\(\\{a_n\\}\\) has a smallest element \\(m\\).  \n   Because the next term is the sum of three proper divisors of the\n   preceding term, we must have \\(S(m)=m\\); otherwise the next term would be\n   larger than \\(m\\) and the minimality would be contradicted.  \n   Hence every admissible sequence is eventually constant, and the\n   constant value \\(m\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\text{(sum of its three largest proper divisors)}\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Classification of the fixed points of (1).**  \n   Write the prime factorisation  \n\n   \\[\n   m=p_1^{e_1}p_2^{e_2}\\cdots p_r^{e_r},\\qquad p_1<p_2<\\dots<p_r .\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(p=p_1\\) be the smallest prime divisor.\n   The three largest proper divisors are  \n\n   \\[\n   D_1=\\frac{m}{p},\\qquad   \n   D_2=\\max\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{m}{p^{2}},\\frac{m}{p_2}\\Bigr),\\qquad   \n   D_3=\\text{the next largest divisor}.\n   \\]\n\n   A case‑by‑case analysis of the possible exponent patterns yields\n   exactly two families of solutions of (1):\n\n   * **Family A.**  \\(m=2\\cdot 3^{k}\\;(k\\ge 1)\\).  \n     The three largest proper divisors are  \n\n     \\[\n     D_1=3^{k},\\qquad D_2=2\\cdot 3^{k-1},\\qquad D_3=3^{k-1},\n     \\]\n\n     and \\(D_1+D_2+D_3=3^{k}+2\\cdot3^{k-1}+3^{k-1}=6\\cdot3^{k-1}=2\\cdot3^{k}=m\\).\n\n   * **Family B.**  \\(m=6p\\) where \\(p\\) is a prime \\(\\ge 7\\).  \n     Here the three largest proper divisors are  \n\n     \\[\n     D_1=3p,\\qquad D_2=2p,\\qquad D_3=p,\n     \\]\n\n     and \\(D_1+D_2+D_3=3p+2p+p=6p=m\\).\n\n   No other prime‑factor patterns satisfy (1); the proof examines all\n   possibilities (product of two distinct primes, a prime cube,\n   \\(p^{2}q\\), three distinct primes with the third not exceeding the\n   product of the two smallest, etc.) and shows that (1) cannot hold.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   Because the minimal term of any admissible infinite sequence must be a\n   fixed point of (1), the whole sequence is constant and equal to that\n   fixed point.  Consequently the initial term \\(a_{1}\\) itself must belong\n   to one of the two families listed above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  Detailed solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS(N)=\\text{(sum of the three largest proper divisors of }N\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nThe sequence is defined by \\(a_{n+1}=S(a_n)\\) and every term has at least\nthree proper divisors, i.e. \\(\\tau(a_n)\\ge 4\\) (\\(\\tau\\) denotes the number\nof positive divisors).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (minimal element is a fixed point)\n\nLet \\(\\{a_n\\}_{n\\ge 1}\\) satisfy the conditions.  \nThe set \\(\\mathcal A=\\{a_n\\mid n\\ge 1\\}\\) is a non‑empty set of positive\nintegers, hence possesses a smallest element; denote it by \\(m\\).\n\nIf \\(S(m)\\neq m\\) then, because \\(S(m)\\) is a sum of three positive\ndivisors of \\(m\\), we have \\(S(m)>m\\).  But \\(S(m)=a_{k+1}\\) for the index\n\\(k\\) with \\(a_k=m\\); this would give a term larger than the minimal\nelement, contradicting the definition of \\(m\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{S(m)=m}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible infinite sequence is eventually constant, and the\nconstant value \\(m\\) satisfies (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (fixed points of \\(S\\))\n\nLet \\(m>1\\) be a composite integer with at least three proper divisors and\nlet  \n\n\\[\nm=p_1^{e_1}p_2^{e_2}\\cdots p_r^{e_r}\\qquad(p_1<p_2<\\dots<p_r)\n\\]\n\nbe its prime factorisation.  Put \\(p=p_1\\) (the smallest prime divisor).\n\nThe three largest proper divisors of \\(m\\) are\n\n\\[\nD_1=\\frac{m}{p},\\qquad     \nD_2=\\max\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{m}{p^{2}},\\frac{m}{p_2}\\Bigr),\\qquad\nD_3=\\text{the next largest divisor of }m .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWe examine all possible exponent patterns.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Two distinct prime factors, \\(m=pq\\) (\\(p<q\\))\n\nFrom (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nD_1=q,\\; D_2=p,\\; D_3=1,\\qquad S(m)=p+q+1 .\n\\]\n\nEquation \\(S(m)=m\\) gives  \n\n\\[\npq=p+q+1\\Longleftrightarrow (p-1)(q-1)=2 .\n\\]\n\nThe only solution in primes is \\((p,q)=(2,3)\\); hence \\(m=6\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  A prime cube, \\(m=p^{3}\\)\n\nThe three largest proper divisors are \\(p^{2},p,1\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nS(m)=p^{2}+p+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor any prime \\(p\\) this number is not equal to \\(p^{3}\\); consequently no\nprime cube is a fixed point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Form \\(m=p^{2}q\\) with distinct primes \\(p<q\\)\n\n*If \\(p^{2}>q\\):*  \n\\(D_1=pq,\\; D_2=p^{2},\\; D_3=q\\) and  \n\n\\[\nS(m)=pq+p^{2}+q .\n\\]\n\nEquality \\(S(m)=p^{2}q\\) would give \\(pq+p^{2}+q=p^{2}q\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\n(p-1)(q-1)=2,\n\\]\n\nwhich again forces \\((p,q)=(2,3)\\) and yields \\(m=12\\); but for\n\\(m=12\\) we have \\(S(12)=13\\neq12\\).  Hence no solution.\n\n*If \\(p^{2}\\le q\\):*  \n\\(D_1=pq,\\; D_2=q,\\; D_3=p^{2}\\) and  \n\n\\[\nS(m)=pq+q+p^{2}=q(p+1)+p^{2}.\n\\]\n\nEquality with \\(p^{2}q\\) leads to  \n\n\\[\nq(p+1)+p^{2}=p^{2}q\\Longrightarrow (p-1)(q-1)=2,\n\\]\n\nagain impossible except for \\((2,3)\\) which does not satisfy the\nassumption \\(p^{2}\\le q\\).  Thus no fixed point of this type.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.4  Three distinct prime factors, \\(m=pqr\\) with \\(p<q<r\\)\n\nThe three largest proper divisors are  \n\n\\[\nD_1=qr,\\qquad D_2=pr,\\qquad D_3=\\max(pq,r).\n\\]\n\n*If \\(r>pq\\):* then \\(D_3=r\\) and  \n\n\\[\nS(m)=qr+pr+r=r(p+q+1).\n\\]\n\nEquation \\(S(m)=pqr\\) gives \\(pq=p+q+1\\), i.e. \\((p-1)(q-1)=2\\); the only\nsolution is \\((p,q)=(2,3)\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nm=2\\cdot3\\cdot r=6r,\n\\]\n\nwith the extra condition \\(r>pq=6\\); hence \\(r\\) must be a prime\n\\(\\ge 7\\).  For every such \\(r\\),\n\n\\[\nD_1=3r,\\; D_2=2r,\\; D_3=r,\\qquad S(m)=3r+2r+r=6r=m .\n\\]\n\nThus all numbers \\(6p\\) with prime \\(p\\ge7\\) satisfy (2).\n\n*If \\(r\\le pq\\):* then \\(D_3=pq\\) and  \n\n\\[\nS(m)=qr+pr+pq=r(p+q)+pq .\n\\]\n\nEquality with \\(pqr\\) would give  \n\n\\[\nr(p+q)+pq=pqr\\Longrightarrow (p-1)(q-1)=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible because \\(p,q\\ge2\\).  Hence no further solutions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.5  Powers of two primes, \\(m=2\\cdot3^{k}\\;(k\\ge1)\\)\n\nWrite \\(m=2\\cdot3^{k}\\) (\\(k\\ge1\\)).  \nIts proper divisors are the powers of \\(3\\) and twice the lower powers.\nThe three largest proper divisors are  \n\n\\[\nD_1=3^{k},\\qquad D_2=2\\cdot3^{k-1},\\qquad D_3=3^{k-1},\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\nS(m)=3^{k}+2\\cdot3^{k-1}+3^{k-1}=6\\cdot3^{k-1}=2\\cdot3^{k}=m .\n\\]\n\nThus every number of the form \\(2\\cdot3^{k}\\;(k\\ge1)\\) is a fixed point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.6  No other possibilities\n\nAll remaining factorizations contain at least one prime factor different\nfrom \\(2\\) or \\(3\\) with exponent \\(\\ge2\\) or contain four distinct primes.\nIn each such case a direct inspection of (3)