e now \\(2,3,5\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(n')=\\frac{31}{30}n' .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(30\\mid n'\\), the quotient \\(n'/30\\) is odd; multiplying by \\(31\\) yields an odd integer. By Lemma 1 this odd term forces the sequence to leave the set \\(\\{n:\\tau(n)\\ge4\\}\\). Hence a starting integer containing the factor \\(5\\) cannot generate an infinite admissible sequence. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequences of the lemmas  \n\n* From Lemma 1 any admissible \\(a_{1}\\) must be even.  \n* Lemma 2 forces the presence of the factor \\(3\\).  \n* Lemma 3 forbids the factor \\(5\\).\n\nHence every admissible integer can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=2^{\\alpha}3^{\\beta}m,\\qquad\\alpha\\ge1,\\;\\beta\\ge1,\\;\nm\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Parity of the exponent of 2  \n\nIf \\(\\alpha\\) were even, applying (3) (see below) repeatedly would eventually eliminate the factor \\(2\\) and produce an odd term, contradicting Lemma 1. Therefore \\(\\alpha\\) must be odd; write  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha=2k+1\\qquad(k\\ge0). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Behaviour when \\(4\\mid n\\)\n\nUnder the conditions (2) and (3) the three smallest proper divisors of \\(n\\) are \\(2,3,4\\). Substituting \\(d_{1}=2,\\;d_{2}=3,\\;d_{3}=4\\) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)=\\frac{13}{12}n}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4) reduces the exponent of \\(2\\) by \\(2\\) and multiplies the number by \\(13\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reaching a fixed point  \n\nStarting from (2) with \\(\\alpha=2k+1\\) and applying (4) exactly \\(k\\) times we obtain  \n\n\\[\na_{k+1}=2^{1}3^{1}13^{\\,k}m=6\\cdot13^{\\,k}m .\n\\]\n\nFor this number the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,6\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(a_{k+1})=a_{k+1},\n\\]\n\nso the sequence becomes constant from this point onward.  \nEvery intermediate term still possesses at least three proper divisors (they contain \\(2,3\\) and either \\(4\\) or \\(6\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction  \n\nFor any integers \\(k\\ge0\\) and odd \\(m\\) with \\(5\\nmid m\\) define  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=6\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m=2^{2k+1}3^{k+1}m .\n\\]\n\nInduction using (4) yields  \n\n\\[\na_{i+1}=6\\cdot12^{\\,k-i}\\cdot13^{\\,i}m\\qquad(0\\le i\\le k),\n\\]\n\nand \\(a_{k+1}=6\\cdot13^{k}m\\) is a fixed point. Hence every such \\(a_{1}\\) indeed generates an infinite sequence all of whose terms have at least three proper divisors.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness  \n\nLemmas 1–3 together with the parity argument on the exponent of \\(2\\) force any admissible \\(a_{1}\\) to have the form (2) with \\(\\alpha\\) odd, i.e. precisely the numbers  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=6\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m,\\qquad k\\ge0,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m .\n\\]\n\nNo integer outside this family can start a valid sequence, completing the classification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=6\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m\\qquad(k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m)} .\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\