ntermediate term still possesses at least three proper divisors (they contain \\(2,3\\) and either \\(4\\) or \\(6\\)).\n\n8. **Construction of all admissible starters.**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\ge0\\) and let \\(m\\) be any odd integer not divisible by \\(5\\). Define  \n\n   \\[\n   a_{1}=6\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m=2^{2k+1}3^{k+1}m .\n   \\]\n\n   Using (4) repeatedly we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a_{i+1}=6\\cdot12^{\\,k-i}\\cdot13^{\\,i}m\\qquad(0\\le i\\le k),\n   \\]\n\n   and for \\(i=k\\) we have the fixed point \\(a_{k+2}=6\\cdot13^{k}m\\).  \n   Hence every number of the displayed form indeed generates an infinite sequence satisfying the problem’s condition.\n\n9. **Exhaustiveness.**  \n\n   Lemmas 1–4 together force any admissible \\(a_{1}\\) to be of the shape (3) with \\(\\alpha\\) odd, i.e. exactly the numbers described in step 8. No other integer can start a valid sequence.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\text{sum of the three largest proper divisors of }n .\n\\]\n\nFor a composite integer \\(n\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\nd_{1}<d_{2}<d_{3}\n\\]\n\nits three smallest proper divisors larger than \\(1\\).  \nThe three largest proper divisors are \\(\\dfrac{n}{d_{1}},\\dfrac{n}{d_{2}},\\dfrac{n}{d_{3}}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)=\\frac{n}{d_{1}}+\\frac{n}{d_{2}}+\\frac{n}{d_{3}}\n      =n\\Bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\Bigr)}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (odd numbers decrease)\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd and has at least three proper divisors, then \\(f(n)<n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(n\\) is odd, its smallest proper divisor exceeding \\(1\\) is at least \\(3\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\n   \\le\\frac13+\\frac15+\\frac17=\\frac{71}{105}<1 .\n\\]\n\nBy (1) we obtain \\(f(n)=n\\bigl(\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\\bigr)<n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (even numbers not divisible by 3 decrease)\n\nLet \\(n\\) be even, \\(3\\nmid n\\), and assume \\(n\\) has at least three proper divisors. Then \\(f(n)<n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(4\\mid n\\) the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,4\\) and the smallest odd divisor \\(p\\ge5\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\n   \\le\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac{19}{20}<1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(4\\nmid n\\) the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,p,q\\) with \\(p\\ge5\\) and \\(q\\ge p\\); then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac1{d_{1}}+\\frac1{d_{2}}+\\frac1{d_{3}}\n   \\le\\frac12+\\frac15+\\frac15=\\frac{9}{10}<1 .\n\\]\n\nIn either case (1) yields \\(f(n)<n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence of Lemmas 1 and 2  \n\nIf an infinite sequence \\((a_{n})\\) satisfies the problem’s condition, every term must be **even and a multiple of 3**; otherwise Lemma 1 or Lemma 2 would give a strict decrease, and after finitely many steps we would reach a number with fewer than three proper divisors, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (the factor 5 cannot appear)\n\nNo term of an admissible sequence is divisible by \\(5\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a term \\(n\\) is divisible by \\(5\\). By the previous consequence we already know \\(3\\mid n\\). Write  \n\n\\[\nn=2^{\\alpha}3^{\\beta}5^{\\gamma}m,\\qquad \\alpha\\ge1,\\;\\beta,\\gamma\\ge1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m .\n\\]\n\nWhile \\(\\alpha\\ge2\\) the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,4\\); inserting \\(d_{1}=2,d_{2}=3,d_{3}=4\\) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\frac{13}{12}n . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach use of (2) reduces the exponent of \\(2\\) by \\(2\\) and leaves the factor \\(5\\) unchanged. After \\(\\lfloor\\alpha/2\\rfloor\\) applications we obtain a number \\(n'\\) whose exponent of \\(2\\) is either \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).\n\n*If the exponent is \\(0\\)*, then \\(n'\\) is odd, contradicting Lemma 1.\n\n*If the exponent is \\(1\\)*, the three smallest proper divisors become \\(2,3,5\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nf(n')=\\frac{31}{30}n' .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(30\\mid n'\\), the quotient \\(n'/30\\) is odd; multiplying by the odd prime \\(31\\) yields an odd integer, again contradicting Lemma 1.  \nThus a factor \\(5\\) cannot occur in any term. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Form of admissible numbers  \n\nFrom the two consequences we obtain that every admissible term has the shape  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{n=2^{\\alpha}3^{\\beta}m},\\qquad \\alpha\\ge1,\\;\\beta\\ge1,\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (the exponent of 2 is odd)\n\nIn (3) the exponent \\(\\alpha\\) must be odd.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose \\(\\alpha\\) is even, \\(\\alpha=2k\\) with \\(k\\ge1\\). While \\(4\\mid n\\) the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,4\\) and (2) holds, decreasing \\(\\alpha\\) by \\(2\\) each step. After \\(k\\) steps we obtain a number with exponent \\(0\\), i.e. an odd integer, contradicting Lemma 1. ∎\n\nHence we may write  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha=2k+1\\qquad(k\\ge0). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Behaviour while \\(4\\mid n\\)\n\nIf \\(\\alpha\\ge2\\) (equivalently \\(k\\ge1\\)), the three smallest proper divisors are indeed \\(2,3,4\\); inserting them into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)=\\frac{13}{12}n}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquation (5) reduces the exponent of \\(2\\) by \\(2\\) and multiplies the number by \\(13\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reaching a fixed point\n\nStarting from a number of the form (3) with \\(\\alpha=2k+1\\) and applying (5) exactly \\(k\\) times we obtain  \n\n\\[\na_{k+1}=2^{1}3^{1}13^{\\,k}m=6\\cdot13^{\\,k}m .\n\\]\n\nFor this integer the three smallest proper divisors are \\(2,3,6\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nf(6\\cdot13^{\\,k}m)=6\\cdot13^{\\,k}m ,\n\\]\n\nso the sequence becomes constant from the \\((k+1)^{\\text{st}}\\) term onward.  \nAll intermediate terms contain the divisors \\(2,3\\) and either \\(4\\) (when \\(\\alpha\\ge2\\)) or \\(6\\) (when \\(\\alpha=1\\)), guaranteeing at least three proper divisors throughout.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction of all admissible starters\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\qquad m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=6\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m=2^{2k+1}3^{k+1}m .\n\\]\n\nUsing (5) repeatedly we obtain  \n\n\\[\na_{i+1}=6\\cdot12^{\\,k-i}\\cdot13^{\\,i}m\\qquad(0\\le i\\le k),\n\\]\n\nand for \\(i=k\\) we reach the fixed point \\(a_{k+2}=6\\cdot13^{k}m\\).  \nThus every integer of the displayed form indeed generates an infinite sequence satisfying the problem’s requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nLemmas 1–4 force any admissible \\(a_{1}\\) to be of the shape  \n\n\\[\na_{1}=2^{2k+1}3^{k+1}m=6\\cdot12^{k}\\,m,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(k\\ge0\\), \\(m\\) odd and \\(5\\nmid m\\).  \nNo other integer can start a valid sequence, because any deviation from this form either violates Lemma 1 (producing an odd term) or Lemma 2 (producing a term not divisible by 3), or introduces a factor 5, which Lemma 3 excludes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{a_{1}=6\\cdot12^{k}\\cdot m\\qquad(k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\;m\\text{ odd},\\;5\\nmid m)} .\n\\]"
